{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs28 Make tracks inactive and active again, like Pro Tools\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works on the selected tracks, or the track under the mouse if none are selected. Making a track inactive sets all its FX offline (track FX and input FX, which frees their CPU and memory), mutes it (which silences its sends too), disarms recording and turns the track grey. Running it again puts everything back exactly as it was: FX that were already offline stay offline, and the track's mute, record arm and colour come back. Only REAPER's own track settings are used, so the project stays a normal REAPER project. Without ReaTooled, an inactive track simply looks muted and grey with its FX offline. If some of the tracks are inactive and some aren't, all of them are made active. The track stays visible; hide it as usual if you want.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs28 Pro Tools-style memory location recall with REAPER markers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Map this to the numeric keypad's "." key. Press [.], type a marker number, then press [.] or Enter: the edit cursor (and playback, if playing) jumps to that marker. If there's no marker with that number, the region with that number is selected (time selection, cursor at its start), like recalling a Pro Tools "selection" memory location. While the window is open, [+] and [-] step to the next and previous marker or region, and the window stays open so you can keep stepping (like Pro Tools' [.] [+] / [.] [-]). As you type, the window shows the marker's name, or says right away that there's no such number. Uses REAPER's own marker and region numbers, so it works in any project. Esc cancels, Backspace deletes a digit. The window closes by itself after a few seconds without a key press, or when you click somewhere else. Needs ReaImGui (installed with ReaTooled).\par}
}

